 ],
 "literal": "Male elephants tied, groves were ruined / Chariots running, dust filled the streets / Horses roaming, paths became obscured / Vessels washing, waterfronts became muddy / Destructive warriors crowding, because of abounding / Weight increased, the earth to sag / Those who came are many indeed, newly arrived kings / Like the sighing of a female elephant, hand-grabbing iron bellows / Resembling a painting, dark back, guarding chaplet / Black eyes having, close-set desirable breasts / Bewildering look, the woman, those who desired / Are pitiable themselves indeed, her brothers indeed / Wealth do not want, desiring battle, seeking / Not of equal status, to those, giving indeed is not there, saying / Tightly bound shields, sharp-edged swords / Crowd having, bloody stench of flesh with / Unwashed heads, black-ferruled long spears / Such warriors, even if mothers, alas / What will become indeed, itself indeed / Cotton fence, this fertile good town",
 "translation": "The groves are ruined as male elephants are tied to them. The streets are filled with dust from running chariots. The paths are obscured by the roaming of horses. The water ghats are muddy from the washing of vessels. Because of the crowding of destructive warriors, the earth sags under their heavy weight. Many are the newly arrived kings who have come. Those who desired this woman—with her black eyes, close-set desirable breasts, and bewildering look—are indeed pitiable. Her brothers do not want wealth; seeking battle, they say, 'We will never give her to those who are not of equal status.' With tightly bound shields, sharp-edged swords, unwashed heads smelling of bloody flesh, and long black-ferruled spears, they stand ready. Alas, even if they are the mothers of such fierce warriors, what will become of this fertile, good town with its cotton fences?",
 "modern": "யானைகளைக் கட்டுவதால் சோலைகள் அழிந்தன. தேர்கள் ஓடுவதால் தெருக்களில் தூசி நிறைந்தது. குதிரைகள் நடப்பதால் வழிகள் கலங்கின. பாத்திரங்களைக் கழுவுவதால் நீர்நிலைகள் சேறாயின. கொடிய வீரர்கள் திரள்வதால் பாரம் தாங்காமல் பூமி நெளிகிறது. புதிய அரசர்கள் பலர் வந்துள்ளனர். கரிய கண்களையும், நெருங்கிய மார்பகங்களையும், மயக்கும் பார்வையையும் உடைய இப்பெண்ணை விரும்பியவர்கள் இரங்கத்தக்கவர்கள். இவளுடைய சகோதரர்கள் செல்வத்தை விரும்பாமல், போரையே விரும்பி, 'தகுதி இல்லாதவர்களுக்குப் பெண்ணைத் தரமாட்டோம்' என்று கூறுகின்றனர். அவர்கள் கட்டிய கேடயமும், கூர்மையான வாளும், இரத்த வாடை வீசும் கழுவாத தலையும், கரிய நீண்ட வேலும் உடையவர்கள். இத்தகைய வீரர்களைப் பெற்ற தாயாயினும், பருத்தி வேலியை உடைய இந்த வளமான நல்லூர் இனி என்னவாகுமோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Makaṭpāṟ Kāñci theme under the Kāñci tiṇai, which depicts the tragic refusal of a girl's hand in marriage to powerful kings, leading to war. An observer describes the siege of a fertile town by several kings who have come to claim a beautiful girl. The girl's brothers, who are fierce and uncompromising warriors, refuse to give her to anyone of unequal status, preferring the glory of battle over wealth. The poet vividly contrasts the delicate beauty of the girl with the destructive military preparation of her brothers and the invading kings. The imagery of the fertile town with its cotton fences being ruined by war elephants and chariots heightens the sense of impending tragedy. Key words like 'தையல்' (woman), 'மறவர்' (warriors), and 'பணை நல்லூர்' (fertile good town) emphasize the conflict between desire, honor, and destruction.",
